{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NTFNzYjBryhrYvQg3HoBWw","type":"tool_result","content":"[109] Ports and processes: `tools/hub-serve.mjs` runs `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` and parses the printed port. It requests `/` and checks that the page title contains \"Dragon Quilt Builders\" before trusting anything. It kills only the PID it spawned. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`. Prefer `file://` and headless Node everywhere else.\n[204] - **Scenes:** `title` (menu and attract countdown), `attract`, `newgame` (name, look), `intro` (story crawl), `play` (`pausable: true`, `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, wheel: false }`), `results` (chapter clear), `credits`, `about` (also openable over the pause menu), `bee` (Quilting Bee, solo or hub), `hubcard` (the game's own AI Hub card).\n[259] - **Level 0 is Backing Cloth** (unbreakable). Below and outside the world is the **Unravel**, a void. Anything falling below z = 8 units, or outside the world, respawns at its last safe position after a 0.6 s \"unravel\" effect. The builder takes 4 damage.\n[263] get(x,y,z) → id            set(x,y,z,id,{by, silent}) → bool (emits blockPlaced/blockBroken, bumps colVer of the column and 4 neighbours)\n[282] - **Zoom steps.** The player's zoom is quantized to `[0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2]`. Apply with `game.setZoom(step)`. Read `input.zoom()` and accumulate it; snap when the accumulated factor crosses the next step.\n[288] - **Visible columns.** Invert the four screen corners to the ground plane at `z = 0` and at `z = H*16`. Take the cell bounding box plus 2 cells of margin, then clamp it. That is about 500–700 columns at zoom 1.\n[289] - **Liquids.** Water blocks are queued separately: `r.queue(..., z*16, drawWater)`. The water top is drawn with `px.blend(.7)` and a 4-frame shimmer, plus side faces next to air. Blocks under water get a blue multiply tint.\n[290] - **Void and fringe.** Behind the world, draw a deep-navy gradient (`r.sky(['#06051a','#0d0b2a','#141238'])`) with drifting cream \"lint motes\" and a faint stitched grid. Edge columns and void holes get frayed fringe threads: 1-px lines hanging 1–2 blocks below the lowest block, swaying with time. These are queued just before their column.\n[291] - **Cutaway** (seeing inside roofs, tunnels and canopies). Each frame, decide `cut = heroLevel + 2` if either of these holds:\n[292]   - there is any opaque block in the hero's column, or the 2 columns toward the camera, at levels `heroLevel+2 .. heroLevel+5`;\n[293]   - the hero has been hidden by blocks for more than 0.4 s (the x-ray silhouette is showing).\n[294] \n[295]   Blocks at `z ≥ cut` within radius 6.5 cells of the hero are skipped. A 1.5-cell band at the edge is drawn every other pixel (`px.polyDither`). A block just below the cut with a block above it gets a \"cross-section\" overlay: a darker top with a diagonal stitch hatch. The cut eases in and out over 0.2 s (radius grows from 0). Turn it off for 0.5 s after the hero leaves cover. Dev toggle: \"Cutaway: auto / always / off\".\n[296] - **X-ray.** The builder, remote players and bosses use `r.actor(..., { xray: { color, actors: false } })`. They show as tinted silhouettes behind blocks (the engine does this through `occluder` queue items).\n[359] - **Belly** drains 4 per in-game hour equivalent (default 1 point every 9 s of real time, about 100 → 0 in 15 min); sprint ×1.5, fighting ×1.2. At 0, HP drops 1 every 4 s. \"Your belly is rumbling!\" warnings show at 25% and 10%.\n[455] 2. For each L, classify every cell in the region:\n[456]    - WALL: opaque solid at L and L+1, or a stair or table object up to it.\n[457]    - DOOR: a door object at L.\n[489]   - **Ghosts:** each unbuilt cell draws a translucent ghost of its target block (`px.blend(.35)`) with a dashed outline; wrong blocks get a red ✗. The HUD shows a panel: \"Quilters' Hall: 37/127\", then the materials still needed (have/need, e.g. Cobble 12/35).\n[593] - **Engine pieces:** `E.Story` (story module) runs conversations as data nodes, with `who`, `say`, `choices`, `set`, `quest`, `done`, `emote` and `event`. One `E.Dialog` instance is skinned as a Dragon Quest-style box: a dark indigo panel with a cream stitched border (dashed 1-px line 2 px inside the edge), a name tab, a portrait at the left, typed text with Animalese blips, and a ▼ bobbing at page end.\n[1460] Each milestone ends with: `node tests/run.mjs` green, `node tools/build.mjs` producing a playable HTML, and a short note in `DEVLOG.md` in the dev folder. Build the **core loop end to end early** (M1–M6 make Act 1 playable), then widen.\n[1478] Parallel work (for teams): after M1's API is frozen (end of M1), M3 (data and crafting), M4 (worldgen) and M8 (audio) can proceed alongside M2. M11 can start with the loopback once the Builder and VoxelWorld APIs exist.\n[1486] | **Voxel rendering in a 2D engine** (sorting, overhangs, cutaway) | The engine sorts single points; overhangs can draw actors over roofs | M1 first, against the diorama with every hard case (§11.2): lift and sink in `_liftOrder`, runs as occluders, x-ray, cutaway. Use filmstrips in 4 rotations | (a) Draw each block as its own queue item instead of runs; (b) if overhang sorting still glitches, always cut away blocks above `heroLevel+2` within the radius, and let NPCs under roofs be hidden by the roof (sink rule only); (c) as a last resort, limit placement so blocks need support below or a neighbour (no floating roofs). Decide by the end of M1 and record it in DEVLOG |\n[1491] | **Shared-world netcode** | Latency, loss, message caps, host or server modes | Keep the Bee world small; voxel edits are reliable and idempotent; snapshots are unreliable with timestamps; a loopback suite with simulated latency, jitter and loss; run the harness early (a skeleton round in M6) | Reduce the Bee round to the build phase plus a single raid wave; lower snapshot rates to 6 Hz |\n[1493] | **Animalese quality / annoyance** | Synth voices can grate | Prototype the test page in M8 day 1; soft envelopes, low volume (.18), a Voices volume setting, text speed | A one-blip-per-two-letters mode |\n[1494] | **10-foot legibility** | Pixel font at 240p | Text at scale 1 or more; a 1080p screenshot review in M9 | Use `res: 'snes'` (taller pixels) for menus only, through the scene `res` |\n300:The renderer sorts by `view.order(x,y,z) = fx*x + fy*y + z*1e-3`. Runs in one column sort bottom to top, and columns sort by depth. For things that stand on blocks or sit under overhangs, the renderer calls `r._map._liftOrder(view, x, y, z, k)` for every queued item above `z > .5`. `VoxelRenderer.queue` sets `r._map = world`. Implement both directions:\n793:9. On `end`: stop input, `audio.mute(true)` and `music(null)`, show \"Round over\", then do nothing more. On `leave`: remove that builder (its placed blocks stay). In **server mode** (`AIHub.server`): run the host simulation with no local builder, camera or input, and skip drawing except a 1-fps static \"Hosting the Bee\" frame.\n1464:| **M0** | Setup | Folder layout, e\n1465:| **M1** | **Voxel core** (riskiest\n1466:| **M2** | Builder and controls | M\n1467:| **M3** | Items, crafting, objects\n1468:| **M4** | World | `generateWorld` \n1469:| **M5** | Rooms and town | Room de\n1470:| **M6** | Residents and story (Act\n1471:| **M7** | Combat, monsters, bosses\n1472:| **M8** | Audio | Every song and j\n1473:| **M9** | UI polish | Title dioram\n1474:| **M10** | Save, dev mode, attract\n1475:| **M11** | Multiplayer | `DQ.Net` \n1476:| **M12** | Finish | Balance pass w","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5176bdbd-8f33-407b-b89e-9fc1c745933d","uuid":"2385d800-11a4-4f50-8fcd-f66987e4324d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:45:22.366Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"[109] Ports and processes: `tools/hub-serve.mjs` runs `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` and parses the printed port. It requests `/` and checks that the page title contains \"Dragon Quilt Builders\" before trusting anything. It kills only the PID it spawned. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`. Prefer `file://` and headless Node everywhere else.\n[204] - **Scenes:** `title` (menu and attract countdown), `attract`, `newgame` (name, look), `intro` (story crawl), `play` (`pausable: true`, `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, wheel: false }`), `results` (chapter clear), `credits`, `about` (also openable over the pause menu), `bee` (Quilting Bee, solo or hub), `hubcard` (the game's own AI Hub card).\n[259] - **Level 0 is Backing Cloth** (unbreakable). Below and outside the world is the **Unravel**, a void. Anything falling below z = 8 units, or outside the world, respawns at its last safe position after a 0.6 s \"unravel\" effect. The builder takes 4 damage.\n[263] get(x,y,z) → id            set(x,y,z,id,{by, silent}) → bool (emits blockPlaced/blockBroken, bumps colVer of the column and 4 neighbours)\n[282] - **Zoom steps.** The player's zoom is quantized to `[0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2]`. Apply with `game.setZoom(step)`. Read `input.zoom()` and accumulate it; snap when the accumulated factor crosses the next step.\n[288] - **Visible columns.** Invert the four screen corners to the ground plane at `z = 0` and at `z = H*16`. Take the cell bounding box plus 2 cells of margin, then clamp it. That is about 500–700 columns at zoom 1.\n[289] - **Liquids.** Water blocks are queued separately: `r.queue(..., z*16, drawWater)`. The water top is drawn with `px.blend(.7)` and a 4-frame shimmer, plus side faces next to air. Blocks under water get a blue multiply tint.\n[290] - **Void and fringe.** Behind the world, draw a deep-navy gradient (`r.sky(['#06051a','#0d0b2a','#141238'])`) with drifting cream \"lint motes\" and a faint stitched grid. Edge columns and void holes get frayed fringe threads: 1-px lines hanging 1–2 blocks below the lowest block, swaying with time. These are queued just before their column.\n[291] - **Cutaway** (seeing inside roof