{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh3W3FDvzTNYFUuPZfeF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UzfLPFE4ctBpc5yGfyqcvW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174740__x7ef5ea/work/PLAN.md","offset":250,"limit":260},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11014,"cache_read_input_tokens":426340,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11014},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5176bdbd-8f33-407b-b89e-9fc1c745933d","uuid":"fb4221c3-39fa-4ee6-8352-f187d5e1bb4e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:46:40.179Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh3W2yrcQBaCnq72De3m","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01UzfLPFE4ctBpc5yGfyqcvW":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174740__x7ef5ea/work/PLAN.md","offset":250,"limit":260}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UzfLPFE4ctBpc5yGfyqcvW","type":"tool_result","content":"250\t\n251\t#### 6.1.1 Data\n252\t- Block size `T = 16` world units (one tile). Axes: x east, y south, z up.\n253\t- Story world: `W = 144, D = 144` columns, `H = 24` levels. Bee world: `48 × 48 × 16`.\n254\t- `vox: Uint8Array(W*D*H)`, column-major: `idx(x,y,z) = (y*W + x)*H + z`, so a column is contiguous.\n255\t- `meta: Map<idx, {rot, hp}>` holds stair rotation and partial damage. Damage heals back after 4 s without hits.\n256\t- `colVer: Uint32Array(W*D)` holds per-column versions for render caches. `topZ: Uint8Array(W*D)` is the highest solid level + 1, kept on edit.\n257\t- **Objects** (furniture, doors, lights, plants) are *not* voxels. They live in `DQ.Objects` with an anchor cell `(x,y,z)` and occupy that cell (doors and portals occupy 2 levels). `world.objAt(x,y,z)` is indexed by `Map<idx, obj>`.\n258\t- Block properties from `DQ.BLOCKS`: `solid`, `opaque`, `liquid`, `hp`, `tier`, `drop`, `tex`, `light`, `shape` (`'cube' | 'stair'`), `placeable`.\n259\t- **Level 0 is Backing Cloth** (unbreakable). Below and outside the world is the **Unravel**, a void. Anything falling below z = −16 (through a void hole or off the world's edge) respawns at its last safe position after a 0.6 s \"unravel\" effect. The builder takes 4 damage.\n260\t\n261\t#### 6.1.2 API (`DQ.VoxelWorld`)\n262\t```\n263\tget(x,y,z) → id            set(x,y,z,id,{by, silent}) → bool (emits blockPlaced/blockBroken, bumps colVer of the column and 4 neighbours)\n264\tsolidAt(x,y,z)  opaqueAt(x,y,z)  liquidAt(x,y,z)   (integer cells; outside the world and below level 0 is void, which reads as air)\n265\ttopZ(x,y)                  standLevels(x,y) → levels L where solid(L-1) and air(L), air(L+1)\n266\tgroundAt(px,py,r,z,step)   highest solid top ≤ z+step under the circle footprint, −Infinity over void (world units; feeds E.Body-style physics)\n267\tceilingAt(px,py,r,z)       lowest solid bottom above z within the footprint (head bumps)\n268\tcollide(body)              push a circle out of blocks overlapping [z+step, z+h) (same algorithm as TileMap.collide, per level)\n269\traycast(o,d,maxT) → {x,y,z, nx,ny,nz, t} | null          3D DDA (Amanatides–Woo) through cells\n270\tpickScreen(sx,sy) → {x,y,z, n:[nx,ny,nz], obj} | null     view ray through the screen pixel (see 6.1.5)\n271\tbounds(view) → {x0,y0,x1,y1}                             camera clamp (as TileMap.bounds)\n272\tT, solids=[] , _liftOrder(view,x,y,z,k)                  the renderer's hooks (see 6.1.4)\n273\tdiff() → [[idx, id], ...]   applyDiff(list)               save and network: cells that differ from the generated base world\n274\thash() → u32                                              tests and net convergence\n275\t```\n276\tGeneration writes `base` (a copy of `vox` taken after `generateWorld`), so `diff()` is a cheap scan. Saves store only the diff (typically under 30 KB).\n277\t\n278\t#### 6.1.3 Rendering: face-sprite blitting per block, queued per column run\n279\t- **Face atlas.** For each `(view id, zoom step)` the renderer pre-renders a small sprite for every material and visible face: `top`, `faceA` (the camera-facing side on the viewer's left) and `faceB` (right). In three-quarter view there is `top` and `front`. Each material gets 4 variants (picked by `hash(x,y,z) & 3`).\n280\t  - Sprites are rasterized per pixel with `E.tones(base)`. The pattern functions are in §8.1. Each face is an exact parallelogram of the projected block face, snapped to whole pixels. All blocks of a view and zoom project to the same shapes, so one atlas serves the whole world. It is rebuilt only when the zoom step changes. A 90° turn keeps iso faces identical, and the renderer only swaps which world face maps to A/B.\n281\t  - Light: top = `base`, faceA = `sh`, faceB = `deep`, following the engine's \"light from the viewer's upper left\".\n282\t- **Zoom steps.** Zoom is quantized to `[0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2]` (default 1). Our own zoom actions (− / =, right stick ↑/↓) step through them with `game.setZoom(step)`. The play scene sets `camera: { zoom: false }`, so the engine's continuous player zoom stays off and the atlas only ever needs these five scales. The chosen step is remembered in our settings (`dqZoom`).\n283\t- **Run queueing.** For each visible column, split its opaque solid blocks into runs `[z0, z1)`. Queue each run once:\n284\t  ```js\n285\t  r.queue(cx*16+8, cy*16+8, z0*16, g => drawRun(g, r, cx, cy, z0, z1), { occluder: true, top: z1*16 })\n286\t  ```\n287\t  `drawRun` blits, bottom to top, each block's visible faces: a side face shows where the neighbour column is not opaque at that level, and the top face shows where the block above is not opaque or is cut away. Then come overlays: crack stages on damaged blocks, contact AO (a 2-px dark band on side faces at a run's bottom where it meets the ground), top-face edge AO (a dark band along an edge whose neighbour is one level higher), and sun-shadow tint on tops that have a taller column within 2 cells toward the light. Overlays use `px.blend(g, a, 'multiply', ...)` with shared per-view overlay sprites.\n288\t- **Visible columns.** Invert the four screen corners to the ground plane at `z = 0` and at `z = H*16`. Take the cell bounding box plus 2 cells of margin, then clamp it. That is about 500–700 columns at zoom 1.\n289\t- **Liquids.** Water blocks are queued separately: `r.queue(..., z*16, drawWater)`. The water top is drawn with `px.blend(.7)` and a 4-frame shimmer, plus side faces next to air. Blocks under water get a blue multiply tint.\n290\t- **Void and fringe.** Behind the world, draw a deep-navy gradient (`r.sky(['#06051a','#0d0b2a','#141238'])`) with drifting cream \"lint motes\" and a faint stitched grid. Edge columns and void holes get frayed fringe threads: 1-px lines hanging 1–2 blocks below the lowest block, swaying with time. These are queued just before their column.\n291\t- **Cutaway** (seeing inside roofs, tunnels and canopies). Each frame, set `cut = heroLevel + 2` when an opaque block sits at a level in `[heroLevel+2, heroLevel+6]` in either of these places:\n292\t  - the hero's own column;\n293\t  - any of the 3 columns toward the camera. Step from the hero's cell by `(round(view.fx), round(view.fy))`, the ground direction that points at the camera; in iso both components are ±1.\n294\t\n295\t  Ordinary 2-high room walls (levels `heroLevel` and `heroLevel+1`) are never cut; the x-ray silhouette shows the hero behind them.\n296\t\n297\t  Blocks at `z ≥ cut` within radius 6.5 cells of the hero are skipped. A 1.5-cell band at the edge is drawn every other pixel (`px.polyDither`). A block just below the cut with a block above it gets a \"cross-section\" overlay: a darker top with a diagonal stitch hatch. The cut eases in and out over 0.2 s (the radius grows from 0). It stays on for 0.5 s after the hero leaves cover (hysteresis), so it does not flicker at doorways. Dev toggle: \"Cutaway: auto / always / off\".\n298\t- **X-ray.** The builder, remote players and bosses use `r.actor(..., { xray: { color, actors: false } })`. They show as tinted silhouettes behind blocks (the engine does this through `occluder` queue items).\n299\t- **Draw cost guard.** No baking hitches: blits are cheap. If a frame queues more than 2,500 runs (extreme zoom-out), draw the far columns' top faces only.\n300\t\n301\t#### 6.1.4 Depth sorting\n302\tThe renderer sorts by `view.order(x,y,z) = fx*x + fy*y + z*1e-3`. Runs in one column sort bottom to top, and columns sort by depth. For things that stand on blocks or sit under overhangs, the renderer calls `r._map._liftOrder(view, x, y, z, k)` for every queued item above `z > .5`. `VoxelRenderer.queue` sets `r._map = world`. Call it before queuing any actor or object: the renderer reads `r._map` at queue time and resets it to null at the start of every frame. Implement both directions:\n303\t- **Lift.** If the item's cell has a run whose top is `≤ z+1` and it is the highest such run, return `max(k, order(center, run.z0*16) + .002 + z*1e-5)`.\n304\t- **Sink.** If the item's cell has a run with `z0*16 ≥ z + 20` (an overhang above its head), return `min(k, order(center, thatRun.z0*16) - .002)`.\n305\t\n306\tAlso provide `world.T = 16` and `world.solids = []` (the renderer reads both). Test with a diorama (§11.2): a hero under a roof, on a bridge with a body below, beside and behind 2- and 3-high walls, on stairs, and in a doorway. Write a filmstrip check in all 4 rotations.\n307\t\n308\t#### 6.1.5 Picking (mouse and touch-free targeting)\n309\tUse the line of sight through a screen pixel, as `TileMap.pick` computes it: P and N from the view rows. Clip to the world box and run the 3D DDA from the camera side. The first opaque or solid block hit, with the entry face normal, is the **break target**. `hit + normal` is the **place target**. Objects take part through `world.objAt` (placed objects count as hit targets, so the mouse can click a bench or a bed). Actors are picked with the engine's `{ pick: entity }` on `r.actor`/`r.shape` and `r.pick(sx, sy)` (the last frame's draws). Actor picks win over blocks when both are under the pointer. Respect the cutaway: cut blocks are not pickable.\n310\t\n311\t#### 6.1.6 Physics (`DQ.VoxelBody extends E.Body`)\n312\t- Builder body: `r = 5, h = 28` (fits under a 2-block ceiling), `eye = 22, chest = 16`, `step = 2`, `gravity = 800`.\n313\t- **`update(dt, world)`.** Run `E.Body.update` (it calls `world.collide` and `world.groundAt`). Then check the ceiling: if `vz > 0` and `z + h > ceilingAt(...)`, clamp `z` and set `vz = 0`.\n314\t  - **Stairs:** in a stair cell the ground is a ramp along the stair's `rot` (0 → 16 over the cell), so walking up stairs needs no jump.\n315\t  - **Water:** in water, speed ×0.55. A column with water 2 or more levels deep blocks walkers like a wall (bodies cannot enter, and a falling body is pushed out to `lastSafe`). The builder cannot swim, as in the source.\n316\t- **Auto-hop** (setting, on by default). Moving into a 1-block ledge with 2 free levels above it, on the ground, for over 0.08 s triggers `jump(215)` with a small forward boost.\n317\t- **Safe positions.** Store `lastSafe` every 0.5 s while standing on solid ground that is not near a void edge.\n318\t- **No block may be placed inside a body.** `Builder.place` checks every builder, resident and monster AABB first, then shows \"Something's in the way!\"\n319\t- **`world.escape(body)`** moves a buried body to the nearest standable cell within 4 cells. It runs after any set() that could bury something (worldgen stamps, Tangle blocks).\n320\t\n321\t### 6.2 Camera and views\n322\t- `game.follow(builder.body, { z: 14, lead: 18 })`, with `game.cam.bounds = v => world.bounds(v)` and `cam.smooth = .12`.\n323\t- **Rotation:**\n324\t  - Q/E keys, or a right-stick flick (`input.flick('x')`), turn by ±90° with `game.rotateView(±90)`.\n325\t  - Cover the snap with a 0.18 s crossfade: grab the previous frame's buffer before turning and blend it out with `px.blend` on the overlay. Play a soft \"whoosh\" (`sfx('whoosh', {vol:.2})`).\n326\t  - Movement keeps working in screen space (`view.screenDirToGround`).\n327\t- **Zoom:** right stick ↑/↓ or `−`/`=` step through the zoom levels (§6.1.3). In play the mouse wheel belongs to the hotbar; it zooms only on the Map screen. R3/R resets rotation and zoom (`game.resetCamera()`, then zoom step 1).\n328\t- **Settings → Camera view:** Isometric (default) or Three-quarter. It switches with `game.setView`; the atlas has entries for both.\n329\t- Cutscenes take the camera with tweens on a proxy focus object and give it back on exit.\n330\t\n331\t### 6.3 The Builder (player)\n332\t- **Look:** chosen at New Game. Look A: spiky hair and a bandana. Look B: ponytail and an apron. Four palette swatches. Name entry (default \"Patch\") on an on-screen letter grid that works with a pad, keys or mouse; typing letters works directly. The builder never speaks (Dragon Quest tradition); choices are shown as menu options.\n333\t- **Rig:** `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.0, build: 'chibi', ...look })`. Its head top must stay ≤ 28 units; assert in a test via `rig.worldOffset` of the head joint.\n334\t- **Stats:**\n335\t  - HP 40 (+10 per Heartpatch, ×3 in the story, so 70 max). Belly 100. Base attack 4.\n336\t  - Equipment slots: weapon, body, shield.\n337\t  - No experience levels. Growth comes from Heartpatches, gear and the town (as in DQB1).\n338\t- **Movement (tunable):** walk 78, sprint ×1.45 (Belly drains ×1.5), accel 900, friction 10 when no input, jump 215, air control 0.6, coyote 0.08 s, jump buffer 0.12 s.\n339\t- **Swing** (□ / LMB / J), using the equipped weapon:\n340\t  - A tap swings a 2-hit combo (`E.Combo(['slash','backslash'], { window: .3 })`): 0.38 s per swing, active from 0.35 to 0.6 of it.\n341\t  - Each swing hits monsters in a 70° arc within 22 units (`E.inArc` with `dz` ±14).\n342\t  - If no monster is in the arc, the swing hits the **break target**:\n343\t    1. It deals the weapon's `blockDmg`; the block's HP is `DQ.BLOCKS[id].hp`.\n344\t    2. Crack overlays show at 33%, 66% and 90%.\n345\t    3. At 0 HP the block breaks: fabric-scrap particles in its tones, a *rrrip* sfx, and the drop spawns.\n346\t    4. Blocks above a tool's tier go *clink*: sparks, no damage, and the prompt \"Too tough for your Darning Stick!\"\n347\t- **Spin Stitch** (unlocked in M5): hold swing for 0.6 s. A charge glow and rising tone play. Release for a 360° `spin` move: 1.5× damage, knockback 180, radius 26. It does not break blocks.\n348\t- **Place** (△ / RMB / K), using the selected hotbar item:\n349\t  - **Block:** set at the place target, if it is in reach (4.5 blocks from chest to cell centre), empty, not inside a body, and inside the world. The block pops in with a squash tween (scale 0.6 → 1.08 → 1 over 0.12 s), a stitch spark and a \"stitch\" sfx.\n350\t  - **Object:** placed with facing = the builder's facing, snapped to 90°. A green or red ghost preview shows validity.\n351\t  - **Usable** (food, Heartpatch, Banner): use it.\n352\t  - A full hotbar is fine; place takes one item from the bag stack.\n353\t- **Interact** (○ / F / RMB on an NPC or object): talk, open a chest, use a station, sleep, man a ballista, travel by portal, pick up the Lost Bundle, plant the Banner (on the marked spot).\n354\t- **Eat:** use food from the hotbar or bag. Belly and HP restore (§7.3), with a munch ×3 animation.\n355\t- **Hurt:** i-frames 0.8 s, knockback 90, flash, `expr: 'wince'`, a rumble of .35.\n356\t- **KO** at 0 HP:\n357\t  1. The `'die'` pose plays, then an *unravel* effect: the rig dissolves into thread particles.\n358\t  2. Granny says \"Oh, dearie, you've come undone!\"\n359\t  3. Fade, then respawn at the Banner (or the start point before it exists) with full HP and Belly 60.\n360\t  4. A **Lost Bundle** stays at the KO spot (map marker) holding 50% (rounded down) of each material stack. Tools and key items are never lost. On Relaxed, nothing is lost. During the siege or boss, KO instead retries from the siege checkpoint (§6.12).\n361\t- **Belly** drains 1 point every 9 s of real time (100 → 0 in 15 minutes); sprint ×1.5, fighting ×1.2. At 0, HP drops 1 every 4 s. \"Your belly is rumbling!\" warnings show at 25% and 10%.\n362\t\n363\t### 6.4 Input, controls, prompts (controller first, keyboard and mouse native)\n364\t\n365\tAction presets (`DQ.Controls.PLAY`, `DQ.Controls.MENU`) are passed to `game.input.use(...)`. The engine keeps the d-pad and arrow keys on movement and menus. The tables below are the defaults; players rebind through the standard Controls screen (`menu.controls` names them).\n366\t\n367\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyphs | Keyboard / mouse |\n368\t|---|---|---|---|\n369\t| Move | Left stick / D-pad | LS / D-pad | WASD / arrows |\n370\t| Camera turn 90° | Right stick ←/→ (flick) | RS | Q / E |\n371\t| Zoom | Right stick ↑/↓ | RS | − / = |\n372\t| Reset camera | R3 | RS click | R |\n373\t| Jump (menus: confirm) | ✕ | A | Space (menus: Enter/Space) |\n374\t| Swing / break (hold: Spin Stitch) | □ | X | Left click (keyboard-only: J) |\n375\t| Place / use item | △ | Y | Right click (keyboard-only: K) |\n376\t| Talk / interact (menus: back) | ○ | B | F (menus: Esc/Backspace) |\n377\t| Hotbar previous / next | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Wheel, 1–8, [ / ] |\n378\t| Sprint (hold; toggle in Settings) | R2 | RT | Left Shift |\n379\t| Builder's Eye (precision cursor, hold) | L2 | LT | C |\n380\t| Satchel (Bag, Map, Journal) | Create | View | Tab / I (Bag), M (Map), N (Journal) |\n381\t| Map shortcut | Touchpad click (button 17, if reported) | — | M |\n382\t| Pause menu | Options | Menu | Esc / P |\n383\t\n384\t- **Cursor modes** (`Controls.cursorMode`):\n385\t  - **Auto** (stick or keys used last). The target cell is the neighbour cell in the builder's facing, rounded to 8 directions.\n386\t    - Break target: the first solid block in that cell at level L0 (feet), then L0+1, then L0−1.\n387\t    - Place target: L0−1 if it is empty (fills holes), else L0, else L0+1, else L0+2. So walls 3 high can be stacked from the ground and pits get filled.\n388\t  - **Mouse** (the mouse moved or clicked in the last 2 s). The target follows `world.pickScreen` under the pointer. LMB swings at that block and RMB places there. The builder turns to face the target.\n389\t  - **Builder's Eye** (hold L2 / C). The builder stands still and a precision cursor appears at the auto target.\n390\t    - Stick, d-pad or WASD step the cursor one cell (repeat .16 s), clamped to reach.\n391\t    - R1 or Space raises the cursor one level; R2 or Shift lowers it.\n392\t    - Swing and place act at the cursor.\n393\t    - On screen: a grid of stitched dots around the cursor and a level number.\n394\t- **Cursor display:** a stitched wireframe cube (12 projected edges, 1-px dashed cream thread, pulsing). The break target highlights the block's faces. The place target shows a 50%-alpha ghost of the held block or object. Out of reach, it turns red and dashed, with \"Too far\".\n395\t- **Glyph icons** (`DQ.Glyphs.draw(g, actionOrCode, x, y)`). The engine font lacks ✕○□△, so the game draws its own 9×9 pixel icons:\n396\t  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#55dca8`, on dark discs.\n397\t  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow letters on discs.\n398\t  - L1/R1/L2/R2/LB/RB/LT/RT as rounded rectangles. Sticks as rings with L or R. Options/Create as pills.\n399\t  - Keyboard keycaps are rounded light-grey rectangles with the key's label from `input.label()`; Space is wide.\n400\t  - Mouse: a mouse body with the left or right button filled, or a wheel.\n401\t  - Which family shows follows `input.lastDevice`: gamepad → `settings.glyphs` (PlayStation default, Xbox optional); keyboard → keycaps; mouse → mouse icons for mouse-bound actions and keycaps for the rest.\n402\t  - Every prompt is built from `game.input.label(action)`, never hard-coded.\n403\t- **Context prompts** (bottom left, at most 3): e.g. `□ Break Earth`, `△ Place Cobble`, `○ Talk to Wren`, `○ Craft`, `○ Sleep`, `○ Man the Ballista`. They change with the cursor target and the hotbar item.\n404\t- **Menus:** all of the game's own screens use `MENU` actions. Mouse hover moves the focus, click confirms, wheel scrolls lists. L1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs.\n405\t- **Rumble:** hits .35, block break .15, boss slam .8 (respects the vibration setting).\n406\t- **AI Hub rule:** while Create (Pad8) is held in a session, ignore Options. In a session, the Satchel opens on Create *release* if Options was not pressed during the hold.\n407\t- **Touch-only devices:** see §6.22.\n408\t\n409\t### 6.5 Items, bag, hotbar, chests\n410\t- **Bag:** 40 slots, stacks of 99. Key items are separate and unlimited.\n411\t- **Hotbar:** 8 slots that reference item ids in the bag, DQB style. A count of 0 shows a greyed slot. Pickups fill an empty hotbar slot with new placeable or usable items.\n412\t- **Pickups:** broken blocks and defeated monsters drop item entities that bounce out, bob and spin. After 0.3 s they magnetize to a builder within 1.5 blocks and are auto-picked with a \"pickup\" blip. A toast shows \"+3 Straw\" (stacked toasts, bottom right). If the bag is full, the drop stays and the toast says \"Your bag is full!\". Drops despawn after 5 minutes.\n413\t- **Chests:** 20 slots. Interact opens a two-pane screen (bag | chest) with \"Store all materials\".\n414\t- **Town Chest:** from Town Level 2, any chest inside the border can be made the Town Chest (interact → \"Make this the Town Chest\"). Residents put gifts in and take materials out of it for Patterns, and Marigold cooks from it. Exactly one Town Chest exists.\n415\t\n416\t### 6.6 Crafting, stations, Notions\n417\t- **Stations:** Pocket (from the Bag tab \"Pocket Notions\"), Mending Bench, Hearth Pot, Thimble Forge. A station screen lists known recipes for that station.\n418\t  - Each row: icon, name, and ingredients as icons with have/need counts. Have ≥ need shows white with a tick icon, otherwise red with a cross icon (counts in text too, never colour alone). The pixel font has no ✓ or ✗, so draw them as `DQ.Glyphs` icons.\n419\t  - Quantity picker ×1, ×5 or Max. Confirm crafts.\n420\t  - At most 6 rows per page. A large detail pane shows the description.\n421\t- **Crafting feedback:** the builder hammers at the station (a `bash` loop for 0.5 s), sparkles fly, a \"ta-da\" chime plays, and the item's icon pops above the station. Crafted items go into the bag.\n422\t- **Notions** (inspiration). Each recipe has `notion: { on: 'pickup'|'quest'|'level'|'talk'|'start', key }`. The first time the trigger fires:\n423\t  1. The recipe unlocks.\n424\t  2. The builder strikes a thinking pose (a `kneel` blend for 0.4 s, then `'cheer'`).\n425\t  3. A thought bubble shows the item icon above the head for 2 s.\n426\t  4. A harp-gliss jingle plays.\n427\t  5. The popup reads \"**You've got a notion!** Straw Bed — craft it at a Mending Bench.\"\n428\t  - Popups queue and never overlap.\n429\t  - The Journal's Notions page lists all known recipes.\n430\t\n431\t### 6.7 Objects\n432\tData is in §7.5. Behaviour:\n433\t- **Doors** (2 levels high, 1 cell): they swing open with a tween when a builder or resident is within 1 cell, and close after they leave. Monsters treat closed doors as walls and attack them (door HP 30/50). A broken door drops nothing and breaks the room.\n434\t- **Lights** (torch, lantern, Glow Patch, hearth, forge, banner): each frame, add `game.lights.add(x, y, z+12, radius, intensity, { color })` for lights within the view plus 2 cells. Torch flames come from the engine's shape `flames`. Torches count as room lights.\n435\t- **Beds:** interact at night (19:00–05:00) with no monster within 8 blocks: fade to black, a \"Zzz\" jingle, and time jumps to 06:00. HP is restored, Belly −10, and an autosave runs. During the day: \"It's not bedtime yet!\" Residents use beds at night (§6.10).\n436\t- **Stations:** interact opens crafting. A station must be inside the town border to count for rooms; it works anywhere.\n437\t- **Pin Trap:** a floor object. Monsters on it take 8 damage every 0.8 s and are slowed ×0.6. Builders and residents are unharmed.\n438\t- **Bobbin Ballista** (HP 150):\n439\t  - Unmanned: during a siege it auto-fires at the nearest regular monster within 10 blocks every 5 s (20 damage); it never targets bosses.\n440\t  - Manned (interact):\n441\t    1. The builder sits behind it and the camera eases toward the ballista's facing.\n442\t    2. Stick or mouse aims within ±60°, with aim-assist snapping to the nearest monster in a 12° cone.\n443\t    3. □/LMB fires a spool bolt: 260 u/s, 30 damage, pierces 1. It counts as a *Tangle hit* on Gnarlgrim.\n444\t    4. Reload takes 2.5 s (a crank animation plus a click-clack).\n445\t    5. ○ dismounts.\n446\t  - When destroyed it becomes a \"Broken Ballista\". Interact with 2 Planks to repair it fully.\n447\t- **Bobbin Portals** (prebuilt at POIs): touching one *discovers* it (a jingle and \"Portal attuned: Corduroy Crags\"). Interact opens a travel list of discovered portals: a swirl transition to the destination, ending in front of that portal.\n448\t- **Plants** (tufts, cotton puffs, berry bushes, flowers, puffballs, dead bushes): object-plants that break in one hit with any tool and drop items (§7.3). They regrow at their original spot after 1 in-game day if the cell is still free.\n449\t- **Ancient Buttons** (S3 collectibles): big golden button objects that glint. Interact to collect.\n450\t- **The Banner of Mending:** interact opens the Banner screen: town name, level, a Cheer bar to the next level, the room list, residents and their mood, and the next unlock.\n451\t\n452\t### 6.8 Rooms (`DQ.Rooms`)\n453\t**The rule** (as in the source): a room is an enclosed floor area of **2 to 64 cells**, surrounded on every side by walls **at least 2 blocks high**. It must have **at least one door** in its perimeter and **at least one light** inside. A roof is optional. The room must be inside the town border to count for Cheer, but rooms are detected anywhere so the HUD can say \"Rooms only count inside the town border!\"\n454\t\n455\t**Algorithm** (debounced 0.3 s after any `blockPlaced`, `blockBroken`, `objectPlaced` or `objectRemoved` inside the border + 2 cells):\n456\t1. Collect candidate floor levels L in the region: levels where a cell has solid(L−1) and air at L and L+1, or an object at L that is not a door.\n457\t2. For each L, classify every cell in the region:\n458\t   - WALL: opaque solid blocks at both L and L+1.\n459\t   - DOOR: a door object at L, set in a straight wall: the two cells beside it along the wall are WALL or DOOR. A door on a corner does not count.\n460\t   - FLOOR: not solid at L and L+1, solid below, an object allowed.\n461\t   - OPEN: anything else, including low walls of 1 block and void.\n462\t3. Flood-fill FLOOR cells (4-connected). A component is a room if it:\n463\t   - never reaches an OPEN cell, a region edge or the border;\n464\t   - has a size of 2–64;\n465\t   - touches ≥ 1 DOOR;\n466\t   - contains ≥ 1 light object.\n467\t4. Classify it by the objects on its cells against `DQ.ROOMS`, in priority order. The first match wins; otherwise it is a **Snug**.\n468\t5. Match it to the previous room set by cell overlap ≥ 70%. Emit `roomFormed` (new, or its type changed) and `roomLost`.\n469\t6. **Cheer** is awarded the first time a room of each type forms in a \"slot\". A room's identity is its type plus a hash of its rounded centre, so rebuilding the same room does not pay again; each type pays at most `maxAwards` times (§7.6).\n470\t7. On `roomFormed`: a big centred banner (\"ROOM COMPLETE! — Bedroom\") in stitched letters, a fanfare jingle, confetti scraps over the room, and a room-name label floating over its centre for 3 s. Residents react with `cheer` poses.\n471\t\n472\tThe overlay \"Dev → Show rooms\" tints room cells by type and shows OPEN leak cells in red with a ✗. Use it to explain failures.\n473\t\n474\t**Leak hints:** when a nearly enclosed area fails, the builder looks at the Banner screen or uses Builder's Eye over it. The HUD names the first missing requirement: \"Missing: a door\" / \"a light\" / \"walls must be 2 blocks high\" / \"too big (max 64)\".\n475\t\n476\t### 6.9 Town: Banner, border, Cheer, levels, Patterns (`DQ.Town`)\n477\t- **Planting.** Use the Banner on the marked plaza spot in Thimbleton. That runs a cutscene: the banner rises, a golden light ring expands, and the **border stitch** runs around the town square.\n478\t  - The border is a square of side `2r+1` around the banner, with `r` from §7.7.\n479\t  - On the ground it is an animated dashed running stitch (cream thread, 2-px dashes moving along the edge), drawn at each border cell's surface height.\n480\t  - In the void, at night, it glows faintly.\n481\t- **Cheer sources:** rooms (§7.6), quest rewards, and Cheer bubbles from residents (+5 each, §6.10).\n482\t  - Collecting a bubble: it flies to the HUD banner icon, which pulses, with a two-note \"ding\".\n483\t  - On reaching a level threshold:\n484\t    1. Town Level up (a cutscene of about 4 s): the banner glows, a new stripe stitches itself onto it, and the border grows outward in an animated sweep.\n485\t    2. A popup lists the unlocks.\n486\t    3. The Lv-up jingle plays.\n487\t    4. The next resident arrives at the following dawn (or immediately if it is daytime, walking in from the east road).\n488\t- **Patterns** (blueprints):\n489\t  - **Pattern data:** `{ id, name, size:[w,d,h], layers: [ASCII rows per level], legend: { ch: blockKey | {obj, facing} }, reward }`.\n490\t  - **Placement:** the player uses a Pattern scroll (key item) and a ghost footprint follows the cursor. Turn it 90° with L1/R1 (pad), or the mouse wheel or [ / ] (keyboard); the camera still turns with Q/E and the right stick. Confirm on valid ground inside the border: flat ± 1 level, no objects in the way (blocks in the way are OK; they are shown as \"remove\").\n491\t  - **Ghosts:** each unbuilt cell draws a translucent ghost of its target block (`px.blend(.35)`) with a dashed outline; wrong blocks get a red cross icon. The HUD shows a panel: \"Quilters' Hall: 37/129\", then the materials still needed (have/need, e.g. Cobble 12/35).\n492\t  - **Building:** placing the right block into a ghost cell counts it, with a sparkle and a short arpeggio. Objects in ghost cells (door, lanterns) count the same way.\n493\t  - **Helpers:** residents with the `helper` job (Professor, Wren, and Mortimer by day) take the needed materials from the Town Chest. Each walks to an unbuilt ghost cell they can reach and places a block with a hammer animation and a \"tok\". Rate: `tuning.helperRate` blocks/min each (default 6). If the chest lacks materials, they say so in a balloon (\"Need Planks!\") and idle.\n494\t  - **Completion:** when every cell matches, a celebration cutscene plays (§7.10) and the reward and Cheer are granted. The structure counts as a special room (\"Quilters' Hall\") if its rules match (§7.6).\n495\t\n496\t### 6.10 Residents (`DQ.Residents`)\n497\t- Each resident is a `VoxelBody` plus a `Humanoid` or `Blob` rig, with a profile from `DQ.NPCS` (§7.8): `job`, `schedule`, `needs`, `voice`, `fighter` stats, and dialog nodes.\n498\t- **Schedule** (hours of the day clock; each block picks a target):\n499\t\n500\t  | Time | Activity |\n501\t  |---|---|\n502\t  | 06–08 | Wake, stretch (`wave` pose), go to the plaza |\n503\t  | 08–18 | Job: see below; between tasks, wander inside the border |\n504\t  | 18–20 | Supper: sit at a Mess Hall stool if one exists and eat (+bubble) |\n505\t  | 20–21 | Socialise near the Banner (talk balloons `note`, `dots`) |\n506\t  | 21–06 | Sleep in a free bed in a Bedroom (+bubble at 06:00); without one, sleep on the ground beside the Banner (mood −1, a \"No bed...\" balloon) |\n507\t- **Jobs:**\n508\t  - **Wren** (gatherer): each morning she puts 3 random basics (straw, branch, cotton) into the Town Chest. In sieges she uses a slingshot (4 damage, range 6 blocks, 1.2 s).\n509\t  - **Mortimer** (guard): patrols the border at night with a lantern and fights monsters inside the border with a club (8 damage, `overhead`). In sieges he leads the defence. He needs a Guard Post to stand watch from (+bubble)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5176bdbd-8f33-407b-b89e-9fc1c745933d","uuid":"2a7b30b7-9ab4-4fb7-8cfd-d409cb363318","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:46:40.336Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174740__x7ef5ea/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n#### 6.1.1 Data\n- Block size `T = 16` world units (one tile). Axes: x east, y south, z up.\n- Story world: `W = 144, D = 144` columns, `H = 24` levels. Bee world: `48 × 48 × 16`.\n- `vox: Uint8Array(W*D*H)`, column-major: `idx(x,y,z) = (y*W + x)*H + z`, so a column is contiguous.\n- `meta: Map<idx, {rot, hp}>` holds stair rotation and partial damage. Damage heals back after 4 s without hits.\n- `colVer: Uint32Array(W*D)` holds per-column versions for render caches. `topZ: Uint8Array(W*D)` is the highest solid level + 1, kept on edit.\n- **Objects** (furniture, doors, lights, plants) are *not* voxels. They live in `DQ.Objects` with an anchor cell `(x,y,z)` and occupy that cell (doors and portals occupy 2 levels). `world.objAt(x,y,z)` is indexed by `Map<idx, obj>`.\n- Block properties from `DQ.BLOCKS`: `solid`, `opaque`, `liquid`, `hp`, `tier`, `drop`, `tex`, `light`, `shape` (`'cube' | 'stair'`), `placeable`.\n- **Level 0 is Backing Cloth** (unbreakable). Below and outside the world is the **Unravel**, a void. Anything falling below z = −16 (through a void hole or off the world's edge) respawns at its last safe position after a 0.6 s \"unravel\" effect. The builder takes 4 damage.\n\n#### 6.1.2 API (`DQ.VoxelWorld`)\n```\nget(x,y,z) → id            set(x,y,z,id,{by, silent}) → bool (emits blockPlaced/blockBroken, bumps colVer of the column and 4 neighbours)\nsolidAt(x,y,z)  opaqueAt(x,y,z)  liquidAt(x,y,z)   (integer cells; outside the world and below level 0 is void, which reads as air)\ntopZ(x,y)                  standLevels(x,y) → levels L where solid(L-1) and air(L), air(L+1)\ngroundAt(px,py,r,z,step)   highest solid top ≤ z+step under the circle footprint, −Infinity over void (world units; feeds E.Body-style physics)\nceilingAt(px,py,r,z)       lowest solid bottom above z within the footprint (head bumps)\ncollide(body)              push a circle out of blocks overlapping [z+step, z+h) (same algorithm as TileMap.collide, per level)\nraycast(o,d,maxT) → {x,y,z, nx,ny,nz, t} | null          3D DDA (Amanatides–Woo) through cells\npickScreen(sx,sy) → {x,y,z, n:[nx,ny,nz], obj} | null     view ray through the screen pixel (see 6.1.5)\nbounds(view) → {x0,y0,x1,y1}                             camera clamp (as TileMap.bounds)\nT, solids=[] , _liftOrder(view,x,y,z,k)                  the renderer's hooks (see 6.1.4)\ndiff() → [[idx, id], ...]   applyDiff(list)               save and network: cells that differ from the generated base world\nhash() → u32                                              tests and net convergence\n```\nGeneration writes `base` (a copy of `vox` taken after `generateWorld`), so `diff()` is a cheap scan. Saves store only the diff (typically under 30 KB).\n\n#### 6.1.3 Rendering: face-sprite blitting per block, queued per column run\n- **Face atlas.** For each `(view id, zoom step)` the renderer pre-renders a small sprite for every material and visible face: `top`, `faceA` (the camera-facing side on the viewer's left) and `faceB` (right). In three-quarter view there is `top` and `front`. Each material gets 4 variants (picked by `hash(x,y,z) & 3`).\n  - Sprites are rasterized per pixel with `E.tones(base)`. The pattern functions are in §8.1. Each face is an exact parallelogram of the projected block face, snapped to whole pixels. All blocks of a view and zoom project to the same shapes, so one atlas serves the whole world. It is rebuilt only when the zoom step changes. A 90° turn keeps iso faces identical, and the renderer only swaps which world face maps to A/B.\n  - Light: top = `base`, faceA = `sh`, faceB = `deep`, following the engine's \"light from the viewer's upper left\".\n- **Zoom steps.** Zoom is quantized to `[0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2]` (default 1). Our own zoom actions (− / =, right stick ↑/↓) step through them with `game.setZoom(step)`. The play scene sets `camera: { zoom: false }`, so the engine's continuous player zoom stays off and the atlas only ever needs these five scales. The chosen step is remembered in our settings (`dqZoom`).\n- **Run queueing.** For each visible column, split its opaque solid blocks into runs `[z0, z1)`. Queue each run once:\n  ```js\n  r.queue(cx*16+8, cy*16+8, z0*16, g => drawRun(g, r, cx, cy, z0, z1), { occluder: true, top: z1*16 })\n  ```\n  `drawRun` blits, bottom to top, each block's visible faces: a side face shows where the neighbour column is not opaque at that level, and the top face shows where the block above is not opaque or is cut away. Then come overlays: crack stages on damaged blocks, contact AO (a 2-px dark band on side faces at a run's bottom where it meets the ground), top-face edge AO (a dark band along an edge whose neighbour is one level higher), and sun-shadow tint on tops that have a taller column within 2 cells toward the light. Overlays use `px.blend(g, a, 'multiply', ...)` with shared per-view overlay sprites.\n- **Visible columns.** Invert the four screen corners to the ground plane at `z = 0` and at `z = H*16`. Take the cell bounding box plus 2 cells of margin, then clamp it. That is about 500–700 columns at zoom 1.\n- **Liquids.** Water blocks are queued separately: `r.queue(..., z*16, drawWater)`. The water top is drawn with `px.blend(.7)` and a 4-frame shimmer, plus side faces next to air. Blocks under water get a blue multiply tint.\n- **Void and fringe.** Behind the world, draw a deep-navy gradient (`r.sky(['#06051a','#0d0b2a','#141238'])`) with drifting cream \"lint motes\" and a faint stitched grid. Edge columns and void holes get frayed fringe threads: 1-px lines hanging 1–2 blocks below the lowest block, swaying with time. These are queued just before their column.\n- **Cutaway** (seeing inside roofs, tunnels and canopies). Each frame, set `cut = heroLevel + 2` when an opaque block sits at a level in `[heroLevel+2, heroLevel+6]` in either of these places:\n  - the hero's own column;\n  - any of the 3 columns toward the camera. Step from the hero's cell by `(round(view.fx), round(view.fy))`, the ground direction that points at the camera; in iso both components are ±1.\n\n  Ordinary 2-high room walls (levels `heroLevel` and `heroLevel+1`) are never cut; the x-ray silhouette shows the hero behind them.\n\n  Blocks at `z ≥ cut` within radius 6.5 cells of the hero are skipped. A 1.5-cell band at the edge is drawn every other pixel (`px.polyDither`). A block just below the cut with a block above it gets a \"cross-section\" overlay: a darker top with a diagonal stitch hatch. The cut eases in and out over 0.2 s (the radius grows from 0). It stays on for 0.5 s after the hero leaves cover (hysteresis), so it does not flicker at doorways. Dev toggle: \"Cutaway: auto / always / off\".\n- **X-ray.** The builder, remote players and bosses use `r.actor(..., { xray: { color, actors: false } })`. They show as tinted silhouettes behind blocks (the engine does this through `occluder` queue items).\n- **Draw cost guard.** No baking hitches: blits are cheap. If a frame queues more than 2,500 runs (extreme zoom-out), draw the far columns' top faces only.\n\n#### 6.1.4 Depth sorting\nThe renderer sorts by `view.order(x,y,z) = fx*x + fy*y + z*1e-3`. Runs in one column sort bottom to top, and columns sort by depth. For things that stand on blocks or sit under overhangs, the renderer calls `r._map._liftOrder(view, x, y, z, k)` for every queued item above `z > .5`. `VoxelRenderer.queue` sets `r._map = world`. Call it before queuing any actor or object: the renderer reads `r._map` at queue time and resets it to null at the start of every frame. Implement both directions:\n- **Lift.** If the item's cell has a run whose top is `≤ z+1` and it is the highest such run, return `max(k, order(center, run.z0*16) + .002 + z*1e-5)`.\n- **Sink.** If the item's cell has a run with `z0*16 ≥ z + 20` (an overhang above its head), return `min(k, order(center, thatRun.z0*16) - .002)`.\n\nAlso provide `world.T = 16` and `world.solids = []` (the renderer reads both). Test with a diorama (§11.2): a hero under a roof, on a bridge with a body below, beside and behind 2- and 3-high walls, on stairs, and in a doorway. Write a filmstrip check in all 4 rotations.\n\n#### 6.1.5 Picking (mouse and touch-free targeting)\nUse the line of sight through a screen pixel, as `TileMap.pick` computes it: P and N from the view rows. Clip to the world box and run the 3D DDA from the camera side. The first opaque or solid block hit, with the entry face normal, is the **break target**. `hit + normal` is the **place target**. Objects take part through `world.objAt` (placed objects count as hit targets, so the mouse can click a bench or a bed). Actors are picked with the engine's `{ pick: entity }` on `r.actor`/`r.shape` and `r.pick(sx, sy)` (the last frame's draws). Actor picks win over blocks when both are under the pointer. Respect the cutaway: cut blocks are not pickable.\n\n#### 6.1.6 Physics (`DQ.VoxelBody extends E.Body`)\n- Builder body: `r = 5, h = 28` (fits under a 2-block ceiling), `eye = 22, chest = 16`, `step = 2`, `gravity = 800`.\n- **`update(dt, world)`.** Run `E.Body.update` (it calls `world.collide` and `world.groundAt`). Then check the ceiling: if `vz > 0` and `z + h > ceilingAt(...)`, clamp `z` and set `vz = 0`.\n  - **Stairs:** in a stair cell the ground is a ramp along the stair's `rot` (0 → 16 over the cell), so walking up stairs needs no jump.\n  - **Water:** in water, speed ×0.55. A column with water 2 or more levels deep blocks walkers like a wall (bodies cannot enter, and a falling body is pushed out to `lastSafe`). The builder cannot swim, as in the source.\n- **Auto-hop** (setting, on by default). Moving into a 1-block ledge with 2 free levels above it, on the ground, for over 0.08 s triggers `jump(215)` with a small forward boost.\n- **Safe positions.** Store `lastSafe` every 0.5 s while standing on solid ground that is not near a void edge.\n- **No block may be placed inside a body.** `Builder.place` checks every builder, resident and monster AABB first, then shows \"Something's in the way!\"\n- **`world.escape(body)`** moves a buried body to the nearest standable cell within 4 cells. It runs after any set() that could bury something (worldgen stamps, Tangle blocks).\n\n### 6.2 Camera and views\n- `game.follow(builder.body, { z: 14, lead: 18 })`, with `game.cam.bounds = v => world.bounds(v)` and `cam.smooth = .12`.\n- **Rotation:**\n  - Q/E keys, or a right-stick flick (`input.flick('x')`), turn by ±90° with `game.rotateView(±90)`.\n  - Cover the snap with a 0.18 s crossfade: grab the previous frame's buffer before turning and blend it out with `px.blend` on the overlay. Play a soft \"whoosh\" (`sfx('whoosh', {vol:.2})`).\n  - Movement keeps working in screen space (`view.screenDirToGround`).\n- **Zoom:** right stick ↑/↓ or `−`/`=` step through the zoom levels (§6.1.3). In play the mouse wheel belongs to the hotbar; it zooms only on the Map screen. R3/R resets rotation and zoom (`game.resetCamera()`, then zoom step 1).\n- **Settings → Camera view:** Isometric (default) or Three-quarter. It switches with `game.setView`; the atlas has entries for both.\n- Cutscenes take the camera with tweens on a proxy focus object and give it back on exit.\n\n### 6.3 The Builder (player)\n- **Look:** chosen at New Game. Look A: spiky hair and a bandana. Look B: ponytail and an apron. Four palette swatches. Name entry (default \"Patch\") on an on-screen letter grid that works with a pad, keys or mouse; typing letters works directly. The builder never speaks (Dragon Quest tradition); choices are shown as menu options.\n- **Rig:** `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.0, build: 'chibi', ...look })`. Its head top must stay ≤ 28 units; assert in a test via `rig.worldOffset` of the head joint.\n- **Stats:**\n  - HP 40 (+10 per Heartpatch, ×3 in the story, so 70 max). Belly 100. Base attack 4.\n  - Equipment slots: weapon, body, shield.\n  - No experience levels. Growth comes from Heartpatches, gear and the town (as in DQB1).\n- **Movement (tunable):** walk 78, sprint ×1.45 (Belly drains ×1.5), accel 900, friction 10 when no input, jump 215, air control 0.6, coyote 0.08 s, jump buffer 0.12 s.\n- **Swing** (□ / LMB / J), using the equipped weapon:\n  - A tap swings a 2-hit combo (`E.Combo(['slash','backslash'], { window: .3 })`): 0.38 s per swing, active from 0.35 to 0.6 of it.\n  - Each swing hits monsters in a 70° arc within 22 units (`E.inArc` with `dz` ±14).\n  - If no monster is in the arc, the swing hits the **break target**:\n    1. It deals the weapon's `blockDmg`; the block's HP is `DQ.BLOCKS[id].hp`.\n    2. Crack overlays show at 33%, 66% and 90%.\n    3. At 0 HP the block breaks: fabric-scrap particles in its tones, a *rrrip* sfx, and the drop spawns.\n    4. Blocks above a tool's tier go *clink*: sparks, no damage, and the prompt \"Too tough for your Darning Stick!\"\n- **Spin Stitch** (unlocked in M5): hold swing for 0.6 s. A charge glow and rising tone play. Release for a 360° `spin` move: 1.5× damage, knockback 180, radius 26. It does not break blocks.\n- **Place** (△ / RMB / K), using the selected hotbar item:\n  - **Block:** set at the place target, if it is in reach (4.5 blocks from chest to cell centre), empty, not inside a body, and inside the world. The block pops in with a squash tween (scale 0.6 → 1.08 → 1 over 0.12 s), a stitch spark and a \"stitch\" sfx.\n  - **Object:** placed with facing = the builder's facing, snapped to 90°. A green or red ghost preview shows validity.\n  - **Usable** (food, Heartpatch, Banner): use it.\n  - A full hotbar is fine; place takes one item from the bag stack.\n- **Interact** (○ / F / RMB on an NPC or object): talk, open a chest, use a station, sleep, man a ballista, travel by portal, pick up the Lost Bundle, plant the Banner (on the marked spot).\n- **Eat:** use food from the hotbar or bag. Belly and HP restore (§7.3), with a munch ×3 animation.\n- **Hurt:** i-frames 0.8 s, knockback 90, flash, `expr: 'wince'`, a rumble of .35.\n- **KO** at 0 HP:\n  1. The `'die'` pose plays, then an *unravel* effect: the rig dissolves into thread particles.\n  2. Granny says \"Oh, dearie, you've come undone!\"\n  3. Fade, then respawn at the Banner (or the start point before it exists) with full HP and Belly 60.\n  4. A **Lost Bundle** stays at the KO spot (map marker) holding 50% (rounded down) of each material stack. Tools and key items are never lost. On Relaxed, nothing is lost. During the siege or boss, KO instead retries from the siege checkpoint (§6.12).\n- **Belly** drains 1 point every 9 s of real time (100 → 0 in 15 minutes); sprint ×1.5, fighting ×1.2. At 0, HP drops 1 every 4 s. \"Your belly is rumbling!\" warnings show at 25% and 10%.\n\n### 6.4 Input, controls, prompts (controller first, keyboard and mouse native)\n\nAction presets (`DQ.Controls.PLAY`, `DQ.Controls.MENU`) are passed to `game.input.use(...)`. The engine keeps the d-pad and arrow keys on movement and menus. The tables below are the defaults; players rebind through the standard Controls screen (`menu.controls` names them).\n\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyphs | Keyboard / mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Move | Left stick / D-pad | LS / D-pad | WASD / arrows |\n| Camera turn 90° | Right stick ←/→ (flick) | RS | Q / E |\n| Zoom | Right stick ↑/↓ | RS | − / = |\n| Reset camera | R3 | RS click | R |\n| Jump (menus: confirm) | ✕ | A | Space (menus: Enter/Space) |\n| Swing / break (hold: Spin Stitch) | □ | X | Left click (keyboard-only: J) |\n| Place / use item | △ | Y | Right click (keyboard-only: K) |\n| Talk / interact (menus: back) | ○ | B | F (menus: Esc/Backspace) |\n| Hotbar previous / next | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Wheel, 1–8, [ / ] |\n| Sprint (hold; toggle in Settings) | R2 | RT | Left Shift |\n| Builder's Eye (precision cursor, hold) | L2 | LT | C |\n| Satchel (Bag, Map, Journal) | Create | View | Tab / I (Bag), M (Map), N (Journal) |\n| Map shortcut | Touchpad click (button 17, if reported) | — | M |\n| Pause menu | Options | Menu | Esc / P |\n\n- **Cursor modes** (`Controls.cursorMode`):\n  - **Auto** (stick or keys used last). The target cell is the neighbour cell in the builder's facing, rounded to 8 directions.\n    - Break target: the first solid block in that cell at level L0 (feet), then L0+1, then L0−1.\n    - Place target: L0−1 if it is empty (fills holes), else L0, else L0+1, else L0+2. So walls 3 high can be stacked from the ground and pits get filled.\n  - **Mouse** (the mouse moved or clicked in the last 2 s). The target follows `world.pickScreen` under the pointer. LMB swings at that block and RMB places there. The builder turns to face the target.\n  - **Builder's Eye** (hold L2 / C). The builder stands still and a precision cursor appears at the auto target.\n    - Stick, d-pad or WASD step the cursor one cell (repeat .16 s), clamped to reach.\n    - R1 or Space raises the cursor one level; R2 or Shift lowers it.\n    - Swing and place act at the cursor.\n    - On screen: a grid of stitched dots around the cursor and a level number.\n- **Cursor display:** a stitched wireframe cube (12 projected edges, 1-px dashed cream thread, pulsing). The break target highlights the block's faces. The place target shows a 50%-alpha ghost of the held block or object. Out of reach, it turns red and dashed, with \"Too far\".\n- **Glyph icons** (`DQ.Glyphs.draw(g, actionOrCode, x, y)`). The engine font lacks ✕○□△, so the game draws its own 9×9 pixel icons:\n  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa7ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#55dca8`, on dark discs.\n  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow letters on discs.\n  - L1/R1/L2/R2/LB/RB/LT/RT as rounded rectangles. Sticks as rings with L or R. Options/Create as pills.\n  - Keyboard keycaps are rounded light-grey rectangles with the key's label from `input.label()`; Space is wide.\n  - Mouse: a mouse body with the left or right button filled, or a wheel.\n  - Which family shows follows `input.lastDevice`: gamepad → `settings.glyphs` (PlayStation default, Xbox optional); keyboard → keycaps; mouse → mouse icons for mouse-bound actions and keycaps for the rest.\n  - Every prompt is built from `game.input.label(action)`, never hard-coded.\n- **Context prompts** (bottom left, at most 3): e.g. `□ Break Earth`, `△ Place Cobble`, `○ Talk to Wren`, `○ Craft`, `○ Sleep`, `○ Man the Ballista`. They change with the cursor target and the hotbar item.\n- **Menus:** all of the game's own screens use `MENU` actions. Mouse hover moves the focus, click confirms, wheel scrolls lists. L1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs.\n- **Rumble:** hits .35, block break .15, boss slam .8 (respects the vibration setting).\n- **AI Hub rule:** while Create (Pad8) is held in a session, ignore Options. In a session, the Satchel opens on Create *release* if Options was not pressed during the hold.\n- **Touch-only devices:** see §6.22.\n\n### 6.5 Items, bag, hotbar, chests\n- **Bag:** 40 slots, stacks of 99. Key items are separate and unlimited.\n- **Hotbar:** 8 slots that reference item ids in the bag, DQB style. A count of 0 shows a greyed slot. Pickups fill an empty hotbar slot with new placeable or usable items.\n- **Pickups:** broken blocks and defeated monsters drop item entities that bounce out, bob and spin. After 0.3 s they magnetize to a builder within 1.5 blocks and are auto-picked with a \"pickup\" blip. A toast shows \"+3 Straw\" (stacked toasts, bottom right). If the bag is full, the drop stays and the toast says \"Your bag is full!\". Drops despawn after 5 minutes.\n- **Chests:** 20 slots. Interact opens a two-pane screen (bag | chest) with \"Store all materials\".\n- **Town Chest:** from Town Level 2, any chest inside the border can be made the Town Chest (interact → \"Make this the Town Chest\"). Residents put gifts in and take materials out of it for Patterns, and Marigold cooks from it. Exactly one Town Chest exists.\n\n### 6.6 Crafting, stations, Notions\n- **Stations:** Pocket (from the Bag tab \"Pocket Notions\"), Mending Bench, Hearth Pot, Thimble Forge. A station screen lists known recipes for that station.\n  - Each row: icon, name, and ingredients as icons with have/need counts. Have ≥ need shows white with a tick icon, otherwise red with a cross icon (counts in text too, never colour alone). The pixel font has no ✓ or ✗, so draw them as `DQ.Glyphs` icons.\n  - Quantity picker ×1, ×5 or Max. Confirm crafts.\n  - At most 6 rows per page. A large detail pane shows the description.\n- **Crafting feedback:** the builder hammers at the station (a `bash` loop for 0.5 s), sparkles fly, a \"ta-da\" chime plays, and the item's icon pops above the station. Crafted items go into the bag.\n- **Notions** (inspiration). Each recipe has `notion: { on: 'pickup'|'quest'|'level'|'talk'|'start', key }`. The first time the trigger fires:\n  1. The recipe unlocks.\n  2. The builder strikes a thinking pose (a `kneel` blend for 0.4 s, then `'cheer'`).\n  3. A thought bubble shows the item icon above the head for 2 s.\n  4. A harp-gliss jingle plays.\n  5. The popup reads \"**You've got a notion!** Straw Bed — craft it at a Mending Bench.\"\n  - Popups queue and never overlap.\n  - The Journal's Notions page lists all known recipes.\n\n### 6.7 Objects\nData is in §7.5. Behaviour:\n- **Doors** (2 levels high, 1 cell): they swing open with a tween when a builder or resident is within 1 cell, and close after they leave. Monsters treat closed doors as walls and attack them (door HP 30/50). A broken door drops nothing and breaks the room.\n- **Lights** (torch, lantern, Glow Patch, hearth, forge, banner): each frame, add `game.lights.add(x, y, z+12, radius, intensity, { color })` for lights within the view plus 2 cells. Torch flames come from the engine's shape `flames`. Torches count as room lights.\n- **Beds:** interact at night (19:00–05:00) with no monster within 8 blocks: fade to black, a \"Zzz\" jingle, and time jumps to 06:00. HP is restored, Belly −10, and an autosave runs. During the day: \"It's not bedtime yet!\" Residents use beds at night (§6.10).\n- **Stations:** interact opens crafting. A station must be inside the town border to count for rooms; it works anywhere.\n- **Pin Trap:** a floor object. Monsters on it take 8 damage every 0.8 s and are slowed ×0.6. Builders and residents are unharmed.\n- **Bobbin Ballista** (HP 150):\n  - Unmanned: during a siege it auto-fires at the nearest regular monster within 10 blocks every 5 s (20 damage); it never targets bosses.\n  - Manned (interact):\n    1. The builder sits behind it and the camera eases toward the ballista's facing.\n    2. Stick or mouse aims within ±60°, with aim-assist snapping to the nearest monster in a 12° cone.\n    3. □/LMB fires a spool bolt: 260 u/s, 30 damage, pierces 1. It counts as a *Tangle hit* on Gnarlgrim.\n    4. Reload takes 2.5 s (a crank animation plus a click-clack).\n    5. ○ dismounts.\n  - When destroyed it becomes a \"Broken Ballista\". Interact with 2 Planks to repair it fully.\n- **Bobbin Portals** (prebuilt at POIs): touching one *discovers* it (a jingle and \"Portal attuned: Corduroy Crags\"). Interact opens a travel list of discovered portals: a swirl transition to the destination, ending in front of that portal.\n- **Plants** (tufts, cotton puffs, berry bushes, flowers, puffballs, dead bushes): object-plants that break in one hit with any tool and drop items (§7.3). They regrow at their original spot after 1 in-game day if the cell is still free.\n- **Ancient Buttons** (S3 collectibles): big golden button objects that glint. Interact to collect.\n- **The Banner of Mending:** interact opens the Banner screen: town name, level, a Cheer bar to the next level, the room list, residents and their mood, and the next unlock.\n\n### 6.8 Rooms (`DQ.Rooms`)\n**The rule** (as in the source): a room is an enclosed floor area of **2 to 64 cells**, surrounded on every side by walls **at least 2 blocks high**. It must have **at least one door** in its perimeter and **at least one light** inside. A roof is optional. The room must be inside the town border to count for Cheer, but rooms are detected anywhere so the HUD can say \"Rooms only count inside the town border!\"\n\n**Algorithm** (debounced 0.3 s after any `blockPlaced`, `blockBroken`, `objectPlaced` or `objectRemoved` inside the border + 2 cells):\n1. Collect candidate floor levels L in the region: levels where a cell has solid(L−1) and air at L and L+1, or an object at L that is not a door.\n2. For each L, classify every cell in the region:\n   - WALL: opaque solid blocks at both L and L+1.\n   - DOOR: a door object at L, set in a straight wall: the two cells beside it along the wall are WALL or DOOR. A door on a corner does not count.\n   - FLOOR: not solid at L and L+1, solid below, an object allowed.\n   - OPEN: anything else, including low walls of 1 block and void.\n3. Flood-fill FLOOR cells (4-connected). A component is a room if it:\n   - never reaches an OPEN cell, a region edge or the border;\n   - has a size of 2–64;\n   - touches ≥ 1 DOOR;\n   - contains ≥ 1 light object.\n4. Classify it by the objects on its cells against `DQ.ROOMS`, in priority order. The first match wins; otherwise it is a **Snug**.\n5. Match it to the previous room set by cell overlap ≥ 70%. Emit `roomFormed` (new, or its type changed) and `roomLost`.\n6. **Cheer** is awarded the first time a room of each type forms in a \"slot\". A room's identity is its type plus a hash of its rounded centre, so rebuilding the same room does not pay again; each type pays at most `maxAwards` times (§7.6).\n7. On `roomFormed`: a big centred banner (\"ROOM COMPLETE! — Bedroom\") in stitched letters, a fanfare jingle, confetti scraps over the room, and a room-name label floating over its centre for 3 s. Residents react with `cheer` poses.\n\nThe overlay \"Dev → Show rooms\" tints room cells by type and shows OPEN leak cells in red with a ✗. Use it to explain failures.\n\n**Leak hints:** when a nearly enclosed area fails, the builder looks at the Banner screen or uses Builder's Eye over it. The HUD names the first missing requirement: \"Missing: a door\" / \"a light\" / \"walls must be 2 blocks high\" / \"too big (max 64)\".\n\n### 6.9 Town: Banner, border, Cheer, levels, Patterns (`DQ.Town`)\n- **Planting.** Use the Banner on the marked plaza spot in Thimbleton. That runs a cutscene: the banner rises, a golden light ring expands, and the **border stitch** runs around the town square.\n  - The border is a square of side `2r+1` around the banner, with `r` from §7.7.\n  - On the ground it is an animated dashed running stitch (cream thread, 2-px dashes moving along the edge), drawn at each border cell's surface height.\n  - In the void, at night, it glows faintly.\n- **Cheer sources:** rooms (§7.6), quest rewards, and Cheer bubbles from residents (+5 each, §6.10).\n  - Collecting a bubble: it flies to the HUD banner icon, which pulses, with a two-note \"ding\".\n  - On reaching a level threshold:\n    1. Town Level up (a cutscene of about 4 s): the banner glows, a new stripe stitches itself onto it, and the border grows outward in an animated sweep.\n    2. A popup lists the unlocks.\n    3. The Lv-up jingle plays.\n    4. The next resident arrives at the following dawn (or immediately if it is daytime, walking in from the east road).\n- **Patterns** (blueprints):\n  - **Pattern data:** `{ id, name, size:[w,d,h], layers: [ASCII rows per level], legend: { ch: blockKey | {obj, facing} }, reward }`.\n  - **Placement:** the player uses a Pattern scroll (key item) and a ghost footprint follows the cursor. Turn it 90° with L1/R1 (pad), or the mouse wheel or [ / ] (keyboard); the camera still turns with Q/E and the right stick. Confirm on valid ground inside the border: flat ± 1 level, no objects in the way (blocks in the way are OK; they are shown as \"remove\").\n  - **Ghosts:** each unbuilt cell draws a translucent ghost of its target block (`px.blend(.35)`) with a dashed outline; wrong blocks get a red cross icon. The HUD shows a panel: \"Quilters' Hall: 37/129\", then the materials still needed (have/need, e.g. Cobble 12/35).\n  - **Building:** placing the right block into a ghost cell counts it, with a sparkle and a short arpeggio. Objects in ghost cells (door, lanterns) count the same way.\n  - **Helpers:** residents with the `helper` job (Professor, Wren, and Mortimer by day) take the needed materials from the Town Chest. Each walks to an unbuilt ghost cell they can reach and places a block with a hammer animation and a \"tok\". Rate: `tuning.helperRate` blocks/min each (default 6). If the chest lacks materials, they say so in a balloon (\"Need Planks!\") and idle.\n  - **Completion:** when every cell matches, a celebration cutscene plays (§7.10) and the reward and Cheer are granted. The structure counts as a special room (\"Quilters' Hall\") if its rules match (§7.6).\n\n### 6.10 Residents (`DQ.Residents`)\n- Each resident is a `VoxelBody` plus a `Humanoid` or `Blob` rig, with a profile from `DQ.NPCS` (§7.8): `job`, `schedule`, `needs`, `voice`, `fighter` stats, and dialog nodes.\n- **Schedule** (hours of the day clock; each block picks a target):\n\n  | Time | Activity |\n  |---|---|\n  | 06–08 | Wake, stretch (`wave` pose), go to the plaza |\n  | 08–18 | Job: see below; between tasks, wander inside the border |\n  | 18–20 | Supper: sit at a Mess Hall stool if one exists and eat (+bubble) |\n  | 20–21 | Socialise near the Banner (talk balloons `note`, `dots`) |\n  | 21–06 | Sleep in a free bed in a Bedroom (+bubble at 06:00); without one, sleep on the ground beside the Banner (mood −1, a \"No bed...\" balloon) |\n- **Jobs:**\n  - **Wren** (gatherer): each morning she puts 3 random basics (straw, branch, cotton) into the Town Chest. In sieges she uses a slingshot (4 damage, range 6 blocks, 1.2 s).\n  - **Mortimer** (guard): patrols the border at night with a lantern and fights monsters inside the border with a club (8 damage, `overhead`). In sieges he leads the defence. He needs a Guard Post to stand watch from (+bubble).","numLines":260,"startLine":250,"totalLines":1613}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UzfLPFE4ctBpc5yGfyqcvW","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
